  "verified": true,
  "summary": "The big 5 V Arduino: ATmega2560 at 16 MHz, 54 digital I/O (15 PWM), 16 analog inputs, 4 hardware serial ports, ATmega16U2 USB-serial bridge.",
  "warnings": [
    {
      "level": "info",
      "text": "All I/O on this board is native 5 V logic: the ATmega2560 runs from 5 V, so its pins output 5 V and accept 0 to 5 V (absolute max VCC + 0.5 V). They are not \"5 V tolerant\" pins, they simply are 5 V pins. Connecting a 3.3 V-only device directly to an output pin can damage it; use a level shifter."
    },
    {
      "level": "warn",
      "text": "Pins 0 (RX0) and 1 (TX0) are shared with the USB-serial chip. Anything connected to them can block sketch uploads and Serial Monitor. Use Serial1-3 (pins 14-19) for other serial devices.",
      "pins": [
        "JD1-1",
        "JD1-2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "level": "warn",
      "text": "SPI is on pins 50 (MISO), 51 (MOSI), 52 (SCK) and 53 (SS) and the ICSP header, not on 11-13 like the Uno. Uno shields that use SPI on 11-13 need the ICSP header or jumper wires.",
      "pins": [
        "JXE-16",
        "JXO-16",
        "JXE-17",
        "JXO-17"
      ]
    },
    {
      "level": "info",
      "text": "I2C is on 20 (SDA) and 21 (SCL), duplicated on the SDA/SCL pins next to AREF, not on A4/A5 like the Uno. Pins 20 and 21 have external pull-ups that cannot be disabled.",
      "pins": [
        "JCOM-7",
        "JCOM-8",
        "JD2-9",
        "JD2-10"
      ]
    },
    {
      "level": "info",
      "text": "External interrupts: attachInterrupt numbers 0-5 map to pins 2, 3, 21, 20, 19, 18 (chip INT4, INT5, INT0, INT1, INT2, INT3). Always use digitalPinToInterrupt(pin).",
      "pins": [
        "JD1-3",
        "JD1-4",
        "JCOM-5",
        "JCOM-6",
        "JCOM-7",
        "JCOM-8"
      ]
    },
    {
      "level": "info",
      "text": "The official pinout PDF marks pins 0 and 1 with \"~\", but the ATmega2560 has no timer output on PE0/PE1 and the Arduino analogWrite() reference lists PWM only on 2-13 and 44-46.",
      "pins": [
        "JD1-1",
        "JD1-2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "level": "warn",
      "text": "Current limits: the ATmega2560 absolute maximum is 40 mA DC per I/O pin and 200 mA through the VCC and GND pins (Atmel 2549Q datasheet, Absolute Maximum Ratings). The datasheet specifies output levels only up to its 20 mA test condition, and Arduino's official pinout gives 20 mA as the maximum per I/O pin, so design for 20 mA or less per pin. The datasheet also limits the summed current of each port group (see its DC characteristics notes)."
    }
  ],
